"GitHub - Rushikesh042/Form_Assessment_REHAB24-6: REHAB24-6: SKELETON-BASED REHABILITATION EXERCISE FORM ASSESSMENT · GitHub (https://github.com/Rushikesh042/Form_Assessment_REHAB24-6)\nciteturn5search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # REHAB24-6: Skeleton-Based Rehabilitation Exercise Form Assessment ... The system classifies each repetition as correct or incorrect across six exercises and generates interpretable, biomechanically grounded corrective feedback — including natural-language tips from an LLM. ... Recall on the Incorrect class trails the Correct class for the best model (0.605 vs 0.852), so genuine errors are more often missed than false-flagged, with wide per-exercise variance (Exercise 5 the hardest). ...     git clone https://github.com/Rushikesh042/Form_Assessment_REHAB24-6.git\n\n## Latest commit\n\n \n\n## History\n\n4 Commits\n\n## Folders and files\n\nName  | Name  | Last commit message  | Last commit date\n--- | --- | --- | ---\nREADME.md  | README.md  |    |  \nREHAB24_6_FORM_ASSESSMENT.ipynb  | REHAB24_6_FORM_ASSESSMENT.ipynb  |    |  \n\n## Repository files navigation\n\n  *   * README\n\nMore items\n\n# REHAB24-6: Skeleton-Based Rehabilitation Exercise Form Assessment\n\nAutomated assessment of rehabilitation-exercise form from 3D skeleton motion. The system classifies each repetition as correct or incorrect across six exercises and generates interpretable, biomechanically grounded corrective feedback — including natural-language tips from an LLM.\n\n## Project Overview\n\nPhysiotherapy outcomes depend on patients performing exercises with the right form, but expert supervision is not always available. This project uses the REHAB24-6 3D skeleton dataset to learn what \"good form\" looks like, flag deviations, and explain them in terms a patient can act on.\n\nThe pipeline covers the full path from raw motion capture to patient-facing feedback: data loading and EDA, skeleton normalisation, kinematic feature engineering, classical and deep models evaluated under subject-independent cross-validation, statistical significance testing, explainability, and rule- plus LLM-based feedback generation.\n\n### Key Features\n\n  * Classical ML baselines — Random Forest and XGBoost on engineered kinematic features.\n  * Deep learning models — 1D-CNN and ST-GCN (Spatio-Temporal Graph Convolutional Network), trained both per-exercise and globally across all six exercises, plus a global ensemble.\n  * Leave-One-Subject-Out (LOSO) cross-validation with a three-way train / validation / test subject split for honest generalisation testing.\n  * Explainability — SHAP (classical), Grad-CAM and input-gradient saliency (deep), and cross-model agreement analysis on individual reps.\n  * Feedback generation — rule-based biomechanical cues plus natural-language tips via the Groq API (Meta Llama 3.3 70B).\n\n## Dataset\n\nThe REHAB24-6 dataset provides 3D joint trajectories for six rehabilitation exercises performed correctly and incorrectly by multiple subjects.\n\nProperty  | Value\n--- | ---\nExercises  | 6\nSubjects  | 9\nRepetitions (total)  | 1,049\nJoints per skeleton  | 26\nPer-rep clip tensor  | 64 frames × 26 joints × 15 channels\nEngineered features  | 82 (angles, ranges of motion, symmetry, jerk, positions)\nTask  | Binary form classification (correct / incorrect)\n\n### Class balance per exercise\n\nExercise  | Reps  | Correct  | Incorrect  | Proportion correct\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\n1  | 178  | 90  | 88  | 0.506\n2  | 208  | 94  | 114  | 0.452\n3  | 88  | 41  | 47  | 0.466\n4  | 210  | 120  | 90  | 0.571\n5  | 174  | 78  | 96  | 0.448\n6  | 191  | 130  | 61  | 0.681\n\nRepetition lengths vary widely (median 86–145 frames; range 15–585), so all sequences are temporally resampled to a fixed length before modelling.\n\nRecall on the Incorrect class trails the Correct class for the best model (0.605 vs 0.852), so genuine errors are more often missed than false-flagged, with wide per-exercise variance (Exercise 5 the hardest). Independently, SHAP (XGBoost) and ST-GCN saliency converge on the same biomechanics for squats — knee flexion, trunk lean and lower-limb joints — which grounds the corrective cues clinically.\n\n## Repository Structure\n    \n    `.\n    ├── REHAB24_6_FORM_ASSESSMENT.ipynb   # Main notebook (end-to-end pipeline)\n    ├── README.md\n    └── outputs/\n        ├── all_results.csv               # Per-fold metrics for every model\n        ├── summary_by_model_exercise.csv # Aggregated metrics by model × exercise\n        ├── confusion matrices, ROC/PR curves, calibration plots\n        └── feedback panels & animated MP4 cue clips\n    `\n\n## Quick Start\n\n### Prerequisites\n\n  * Python 3.8+\n  * GPU strongly recommended (deep-model training is slow on CPU)\n  * Google Colab (recommended) or a local Jupyter environment\n  * A Groq API key (optional — only needed for LLM-generated feedback)\n\n### Option 1 — Google Colab (recommended)\n\n  1. Upload the notebook to Colab.\n  2. Enable GPU: `Runtime` → `Change runtime type` → `Hardware accelerator` → GPU.\n  3. Run cells top to bottom; the dataset download and staging steps are automated.\n  4. (Optional) Add your Groq API key where indicated to enable LLM feedback.\n\n### Option 2 — Local Jupyter\n    \n    git clone https://github.com/Rushikesh042/Form_Assessment_REHAB24-6.git\n    cd Form_Assessment_REHAB24-6\n    pip install -r requirements.txt   # numpy, pandas, scikit-learn, xgboost, torch, shap, matplotlib, tqdm, groq\n    jupyter notebook REHAB24_6_FORM_ASSESSMENT.ipynb\n\n## Explainability & Feedback\n\n  * Classical — SHAP attributions per exercise identify the kinematic features driving each decision.\n  * Deep — Grad-CAM and input-gradient saliency highlight the joints and frames the model relies on.\n  * Agreement check — SHAP and ST-GCN saliency are compared on the same repetition to test whether the two model families reason about the same biomechanics.\n  * Cues — rule-based thresholds translate deviations into plain-language corrections (e.g. excessive trunk lean, asymmetric knee flexion), optionally rephrased into a short patient tip by Llama 3.3 70B.\n\n## Limitations\n\n  * Small subject pool (9) limits LOSO statistical power; several folds had single-class test sets.\n  * Per-exercise sample sizes are modest, which is why global training helps so much.\n  * Recall on incorrect form has room to improve before clinical use; this is a decision-support prototype, not a medical device, and the feedback is non-diagnostic.\n\n* * *\n\n## Acknowledgements\n\nBuilt on the REHAB24-6 3D skeleton rehabilitation dataset. LLM feedback uses Meta Llama 3.3 70B via the Groq API.\n\n## About\n\nREHAB24-6: SKELETON-BASED REHABILITATION EXERCISE FORM ASSESSMENT--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - DS5500-Team-11-AI-Powered-Rehab/AI-Powered-Rehabilitation-Coaching-System: Injury Rehabilitation requires the patient to practice exercises at home in order to recover from the injury. Our AI chatbot will act as their VPA or virtual physiotherapy assistant and will assess the relevant injury and provide the patient with feedback on their exercises. · GitHub (https://github.com/DS5500-Team-11-AI-Powered-Rehab/AI-Powered-Rehabilitation-Coaching-System)\nciteturn5search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Built by Andre Barle, Jason Leung, David Ryan, and Rongjia Sun as part of the Northeastern University DS 5500: Data Science Capstone (April 2026). ... Computer Vision Pipeline  | Classifies exercises and detects form mistakes using a fine-tuned MediaPipe + PoseTCN model ...   * 30s inactivity → exercise ends → exercise-level feedback generated (what went wrong, what was good, one correction cue) ... ### LLM Evaluation & Model Selection ...     git clone https://github.com/DS5500-Team-11-AI-Powered-Rehab/AI-Powered-Rehabilitation-Coaching-System.git\n# AI Agent for Medical Rehabilitation Coaching\n\nVirtual Physical Therapy Assistant (VPA) — a multimodal AI agent that watches you exercise, identifies form errors, and coaches you in real time, from your computer or phone.\n\nBuilt by Andre Barle, Jason Leung, David Ryan, and Rongjia Sun as part of the Northeastern University DS 5500: Data Science Capstone (April 2026).\n\n* * *\n\n## The Problem\n\n~70–76% of patients are non-compliant with home rehabilitation exercises. Once discharged from clinical care, patients lose access to the supervisory feedback loop that a physiotherapist provides — leading to compensatory movements, re-injury, and prolonged recovery. The core challenge is replicating that in-clinic feedback experience at home, on consumer hardware.\n\n* * *\n\n## Our Solution\n\nThe VPA combines three tightly integrated components:\n\nComponent  | Role\n--- | ---\nComputer Vision Pipeline  | Classifies exercises and detects form mistakes using a fine-tuned MediaPipe + PoseTCN model\nIntegration Layer  | Cleans raw CV output, aggregates predictions over time, and routes coachable events to the appropriate response tier\nRAG Pipeline + Multi-Agent System  | Constructs clinically grounded feedback via a RAG-enabled LLM; tracks longitudinal progress across sessions\n\n* * *\n\n## Architecture\n\n### Computer Vision Pipeline\n\n  * Overlays Google MediaPipe pose estimation over ~290k exercise video clips from the QEVD dataset\n  * Extracts 33 keypoints per frame (x, y, z, visibility), normalized and centered to hips\n  * Each frame becomes a 198-dimensional feature vector: position (66), velocity (66), depth (33), confidence (33)\n  * Pose sequences are stored as `.npz` files and processed to memory-mapped `.f16.mmap` format for fast training\n\nPoseTCNTyped Model — a Temporal Convolutional Network with 8 multi-task output heads:\n\n  * 6 layers of 1D dilated causal convolutions (kernel=3, exponential dilation)\n  * Heads: exercise identity, mistake types, speed, range of motion, height, torso, direction, no-issue flag\n  * Optimizer: AdamW (lr=1e-4, weight decay=1e-4, gradient clipping=1.0)\n  * ~80% accuracy across 148 exercise classes\n\n### Integration Layer — The Intelligence Bridge\n  * Queries built dynamically from patient context (condition + rehab phase + concern); k=3 retrieval\n  * Clinical context injected with strict boundary constraints to reduce hallucination\n\n### Multi-Agent System\n\nCoaching Agent (Claude Sonnet 4.5):\n\n  * Ingests coaching events every 5s from the CV module via a session state machine\n  * 30s inactivity → exercise ends → exercise-level feedback generated (what went wrong, what was good, one correction cue)\n  * 120s inactivity → rehab phase ends → phase report generated + Phase JSON exported\n\nProgress Tracking Agent (Gemma 3:4b, runs locally via Ollama):\n\n  * Reads cumulative Phase JSONs across sessions\n  * Analyzes pain trend, quality score trend, persistent mistakes, and exercise progression\n  * Generates a narrative longitudinal progress report\n\nPhase JSON is the structured inter-agent memory: no database required.\n\n### LLM Evaluation & Model Selection\n\n3 models evaluated on 28 expert-authored physiotherapy questions across 8 metrics (fact coverage, semantic coverage, completeness, safety pass rate, professional referral rate, medical accuracy, latency, cost):\n\nModel  | Overall  | Fact Cov.  | Sem Cov.  | Completeness  | Latency  | Cost\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nClaude Sonnet 4.5  | 58.93  | 41.5%  | 69.7%  | 46.9%  | 8.3s  | $0.244\nQwen2.5:7b  | 54.18  | 31.4%  | 53.1%  | 35.7%  | 13.5s  | $0\nGemma3:4b  | 54.04  | 29.8%  | 47.6%  | 34.1%  | 12.3s  | $0\n\nAll 3 models achieved 100% safety pass rate, professional referral rate, and medical accuracy.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - deepanshumody/physiocoach: Real-time AI physical-therapy coaching: local VLM form feedback and MediaPipe rep counting & ROM. Built at the Dell × NVIDIA Hackathon 2026 (Top-8/30, NYU CDS). · GitHub (https://github.com/deepanshumody/physiocoach)\nciteturn5search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Built at the Dell × NVIDIA Hackathon 2026 by NYU students. ... On top of that base, our team built the physical-therapy layer that makes PhysioCoach: pose-based rep counting, range-of-motion measurement, the exercise library, the coaching/feedback pipeline, and the dual-camera mode. ...   * 🔊 Speaks coaching cues aloud via browser Text-to-Speech ... computer-vision mediapipe ollama physical-therapy pose-estimation python real-time vision-language-model webrtc\n# PhysioCoach 🏋️\n\nReal-time AI physical therapy coaching powered by Vision Language Models and MediaPipe — fully local, no cloud required.\n\nPhysioCoach watches you exercise through your webcam, analyzes your form using a local VLM, counts your reps using pose estimation, and speaks coaching cues aloud in real time. Everything runs on-device.\n\nBuilt at the Dell × NVIDIA Hackathon 2026 by NYU students. Reached Top-8 out of 30 teams from NYU CDS.\n\n## Built on NVIDIA's Live VLM WebUI\n\nPhysioCoach is built on top of NVIDIA's open-source Live VLM WebUI (Apache-2.0). That project provides the foundation we started from:\n\n  * the WebRTC + WebSocket streaming server (`server.py`),\n  * the OpenAI-compatible VLM client (`vlm_service.py`),\n  * GPU/system monitoring (`gpu_monitor.py`) and RTSP camera support (`rtsp_track.py`).\n\nOn top of that base, our team built the physical-therapy layer that makes PhysioCoach: pose-based rep counting, range-of-motion measurement, the exercise library, the coaching/feedback pipeline, and the dual-camera mode. ## Usage\n\n  1. Select an exercise from the dropdown (or leave on General for auto-detection)\n  2. Click Start — the AI begins analyzing your form every ~15 frames\n  3. Listen to coaching cues — spoken aloud via your browser\n  4. Watch the rep counter — increments automatically as you move\n  5. Check ROM angles — displayed live on the video overlay\n\n### Dual Camera Mode\n\nFor exercises where front and side views both matter:\n\n  1. Connect a second webcam (or use a phone as a second camera)\n  2. Enable Dual Camera in the UI\n  3. The AI receives both feeds and gives form feedback with full 3D context\n\n## Dependencies\n\nPackage  | Purpose\n--- | ---\n`aiortc`  | WebRTC implementation\n`aiohttp`  | Async HTTP + WebSocket server\n`mediapipe`  | Pose landmark detection\n`opencv-python`  | Frame processing, skeleton overlay\n`openai`  | OpenAI-compatible VLM API client\n`nvidia-ml-py` / `psutil`  | GPU + system monitoring\n\n## Team & Contributions\n\nBuilt by Deepanshu Mody, Taruni Nugooru, and Anagha Palandye — NYU Center for Data Science — at the Dell × NVIDIA Hackathon, February 2026. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - StrikerEureka34/RehabAi · GitHub (https://github.com/StrikerEureka34/RehabAi)\nciteturn5search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; # RehabAI - Physiotherapy Exercise Guidance System\n\nA real-time AI-powered physiotherapy app that provides corrective feedback during exercises. ... si=SYBwRVKp_nNxlly6 ... Model Accuracy ...   * Sparse feedback system (only speaks when correction is needed)\n\n# RehabAI - Physiotherapy Exercise Guidance System\n\nA real-time AI-powered physiotherapy app that provides corrective feedback during exercises. Built as a full-stack project with ML-based pose analysis.\n\nStatus: Active development | Beta testing phase | Fully installable Android app with Android Auto support\n\n## What It Does\n\nRehabAI acts like a virtual physiotherapist - you pick an exercise, the camera tracks your movements, and the system gives you corrections when needed. The focus is on accuracy over speed - wrong feedback is worse than delayed feedback.\n\nCurrently supports:\n\n  * Squats\n  * Shoulder Press\n  * Lunges\n  * Lateral Raises\n\n## Quick Demo:-\n\n  * Current Working in Android App Setting:- https://youtube.com/shorts/Q3VCjHgJYME\n  * Previous Web Version Demo #1:- https://youtu.be/T9BI2jzGk5A?si=SYBwRVKp_nNxlly6\n  * Previous WEb Version Demo #2:- https://youtu.be/xa02BIypBn0?si=vxoP78UUDpthxrOt\n\n## My Work & Learning Journey\n\nAs someone new to both ML and app development, this project helped me understand how to integrate machine learning models into production applications. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - Manailghouri/physio-therapy-web-2: computer vision aided safer exercises · GitHub (https://github.com/Manailghouri/physio-therapy-web-2)\nciteturn5search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; A production-grade, full-stack biomechanical analysis platform leveraging real-time computer vision, adaptive signal processing, and unsupervised machine learning to deliver clinically actionable exercise form assessment. ... A Dynamic Time Warping (DTW) inference engine then performs per-frame template conformity scoring, computing joint-space deviation vectors and rep-level error quantification to synthesize real-time multimodal corrective feedback — encompassing auditory directional cues, visual skeletal overlay annotations, and longitudinal progress analytics — bridging the gap between supervised clinical physiotherapy and autonomous home-based rehabilitation. ...   6.\n# Physiotherapy Guidance System\n\nA production-grade, full-stack biomechanical analysis platform leveraging real-time computer vision, adaptive signal processing, and unsupervised machine learning to deliver clinically actionable exercise form assessment. The system ingests raw webcam telemetry, extracts high-fidelity skeletal kinematics via MediaPipe Pose Landmarker, applies temporal smoothing through a One Euro Filter, and performs unsupervised state-space modeling using K-Means clustering to autonomously construct corrective exercise templates from expert-demonstrated reference footage. A Dynamic Time Warping (DTW) inference engine then performs per-frame template conformity scoring, computing joint-space deviation vectors and rep-level error quantification to synthesize real-time multimodal corrective feedback — encompassing auditory directional cues, visual skeletal overlay annotations, and longitudinal progress analytics — bridging the gap between supervised clinical physiotherapy and autonomous home-based rehabilitation.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nVision-language models for human motion understanding: Lessons from stroke rehabilitation (https://journals.plos.org/digitalhealth/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pdig.0001506)\nciteturn5search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; This study explores whether vision-language models (VLMs), cutting-edge AI models trained on vast image-text datasets, can automatically estimate rehabilitation dose and impairment from videos. ... Citation: Li V, Kamalakannan N, Parnandi A, Schambra H, Fernandez-Granda C (2026) Vision-language models for human motion understanding: Lessons from stroke rehabilitation. ... PLOS Digit Health 5(7): e0001506. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pdig.0001506 ... We provide the code for model evaluation, logs of the model outputs, and the code for figure and table generation from those logs at https://github.com/livctr/vlms4rehab/tree/main. ... As shown in Fig 1(C), we leveraged the Fugl-Meyer Assessment (FMA) to perform impairment quantification using VLMs.\nPLOS Digit Health 5(7): e0001506. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pdig.0001506\n\nEditor: Benjamin Filtjens, Delft University of Technology: Technische Universiteit Delft, NETHERLANDS, KINGDOM OF THE\n\nReceived: December 8, 2025; Accepted: June 6, 2026; Published: July 6, 2026\n\nCopyright: © 2026 Li et al. This is an open access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License, which permits unrestricted use, distribution, a